WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.830
Lasso and Ridge regression are similar,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.950
but there's a big, important difference.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.310
We'll talk about it.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.870
StatQuest

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.230
Hello, I'm Josh Starmer and welcome to

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.590
StatQuest. Today, we're going to do part

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.270
two of our series on regularization.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.630
We're going to talk about Lasso

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.630
regression, and it's going to be clearly

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.470
explained.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.750
This StatQuest follows up on the one on

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.710
Ridge regression. So, if you aren't

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.590
already familiar with that, check it

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.710
out.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.630
Even if you are familiar with Ridge

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.390
regression, you should seriously

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.790
consider watching, or at least skimming,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.070
that StatQuest because the examples in

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.990
this video are based on the ones in that

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.150
video.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.510
Lasso regression is very, very similar

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.550
to Ridge regression, but it has some

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.830
very, very important differences.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.830
To understand those similarities and

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.990
differences, let's first do a super

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.350
quick review of Ridge regression.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.990
In the StatQuest on Ridge regression, we

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.830
started out with weight and size

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.430
measurements from a bunch of mice,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.510
and we split the data into two sets.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.350
The red dots were training data,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.270
and the green dots were testing data.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.750
Then we fit a line to the training data

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.470
using least squares.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.190
In other words, we minimized the sum of

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.990
the squared residuals.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.190
When we did this, we saw that even

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.910
though the line fit the training data

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.750
really well, that is to say, it had low

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.870
bias,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.910
it did not fit the testing data very

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.870
well at all. That is to say, it had high

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.190
variance.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.150
Then we fit a line to the data using

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.110
Ridge regression.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.150
We minimized the sum of the squared

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.870
residuals plus lambda times the slope

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.790
squared.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.270
Ridge regression is just least squares

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.270
plus the ridge regression penalty.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.029
The blue ridge regression line did not

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.389
fit the training data as well as the red

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:12.590
least squares line.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.030
In other words, ridge regression had

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.270
more bias than least squares.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.390
But in return for that small amount of

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.790
bias, the ridge regression line had a

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.270
significant drop in variance.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.550
The main idea was that by starting with

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.270
a slightly worse fit, ridge regression

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.949
provided better long-term predictions.

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:37.150
Bam.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.230
Now, let's go back to the equation that

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.590
ridge regression minimizes

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.350
and focus on the ridge regression

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.830
penalty.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.190
If, instead of squaring the slope,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.790
we take the absolute value,

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.870
then we have lasso regression.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.870
Note, just like with ridge regression,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.229
lambda can be any value from zero to

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:01.670
positive infinity and is determined

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.190
using cross-validation.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.350
Like ridge regression, lasso regression,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.870
the orange line, results in a line with

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.870
a little bit of bias,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.470
but less variance than least squares.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.310
Bam.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.910
Ridge regression and lasso regression

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.229
look very similar.

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:25.870
And they do similar things.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.229
In this case, they make our predictions

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:30.590
of size less sensitive to this tiny

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.150
training data set.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.590
Both ridge and lasso regression can be

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.030
applied in the same context, like this

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.070
situation where we are using two

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.350
different diets to predict size.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.430
Or in a logistic regression setting

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.870
where we use weight to predict obesity.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.470
And both ridge and lasso regression can

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.310
be applied to complicated models that

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.550
combine different types of data.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.630
In this case, we've combined the data

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.910
from the first two examples. Weight,

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.430
which is continuous, and high-fat diet,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.030
which is discrete.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.310
Just like the ridge regression penalty,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.350
the lasso regression penalty contains

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.790
all of the estimated parameters except

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.270
for the Y intercept.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.069
It's also worth mentioning that when

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:19.069
ridge and lasso regression shrink

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.110
parameters, they don't have to shrink

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.870
them all equally.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.110
For example, if these were the training

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.590
data,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.630
and these were the testing data,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.030
then when lambda equals zero, we would

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.390
start with these least squares estimates

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.070
for the slope and the offset for diet

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.190
difference.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.870
But as we increase the value for lambda,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.510
ridge and lasso regression may shrink

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.510
diet difference a lot more than they

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.670
shrink the slope.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.430
Okay, we've seen how ridge and lasso

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.350
regression are similar.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.190
Now let's talk about the big difference

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.670
between them.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.510
To see what makes lasso regression

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.670
different from ridge regression, let's

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.830
go back to the two sample training data.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.630
And let's focus on what happens when we

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.909
increase the value for lambda.

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:15.430
When lambda equals zero, then the lasso

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.430
regression line will be the same as the

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.310
least squares line.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.070
As lambda increases in value, the slope

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.230
gets smaller

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.830
until the slope equals zero.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.070
Bam!

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.110
The big difference between ridge and

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.190
lasso regression is that ridge

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.390
regression can only shrink the slope

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.870
asymptotically close to zero, while

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.990
lasso regression can shrink the slope

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.750
all the way to zero.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.230
To appreciate this difference, let's

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:53.270
look at a big, huge, crazy equation.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.430
The goal of this equation is to predict

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.350
size.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.910
The terms for weight and high-fat diet

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:01.950
are both reasonable things to use to

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.110
predict size.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.710
But the astrological sign and the air

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.750
speed of a swallow, African or European,

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.470
are terrible ways to predict size.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.110
When we apply ridge regression to this

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.550
equation, we find the minimal sum of the

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.550
squared residuals plus the ridge

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.950
regression penalty.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.190
And the larger we make lambda,

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:26.950
these parameters might shrink a little

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.710
bit,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.110
and these parameters might shrink a lot,

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.630
but they will never be equal to zero.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.710
In contrast, with lasso regression,

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.909
when we increase the value for lambda,

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:43.390
then these parameters will shrink a

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.110
little bit,

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.350
and these parameters will go all the way

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.030
to zero.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.630
And these terms go away.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.310
And we're left with a way to predict

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.950
size that only includes weight and diet.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.950
And excludes all of the silly stuff.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.070
Since lasso regression can exclude

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.590
useless variables from equations, it is

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.510
a little better than ridge regression at

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:12.430
reducing the variance in models that

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.870
contain a lot of useless variables.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.430
In contrast, ridge regression tends to

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:20.510
do a little better when most variables

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.510
are useful.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.470
Double bam.

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.350
In summary,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.350
ridge regression is very similar to

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.070
lasso regression.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.990
And the superficial difference is that

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.710
ridge regression squares the variables,

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.990
and lasso regression takes the absolute

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.430
value.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:43.710
But the big

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:45.990
is that lasso regression can exclude

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.070
useless variables from equations.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.470
This makes the final equation simpler

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.630
and easier to interpret.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.710
Hooray! We've made it to the end of

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.310
another exciting StatQuest. If you like

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.190
this StatQuest and want to see more,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.110
please subscribe. And if you want to

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.990
support StatQuest, well, please consider

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.550
buying one or two of my original songs.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.520
All right. Until next time, quest on.

